  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Simplifying and doubling a surd side length of a square display area</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A school is preparing a square-shaped display area for a mathematics exhibition. The side length of the display area is \(\sqrt{{@n@}}\text{ cm}\). If the side length is doubled, express the new length in its simplest form.</p>
<p>Your answer should be written in the form \(a\sqrt{b}\), where \(b\) has no perfect-square factors. <i>(Note: Write <code>2*sqrt(3)</code> to represent \(2\sqrt{3}\).)</i></p>
<p>New length = [[input:ans1]] \(\text{cm}\) [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Step 1: Double the original side length</b><br>
If the original side length is \(\sqrt{{@n@}}\text{ cm}\), then doubling it gives:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \text{New length} = 2\sqrt{{@n@}}\text{ cm}\)</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Simplify \(\sqrt{{@n@}}\) using its largest perfect-square factor</b><br>
Since \({@n@} = {@a_sq@} \times {@b@}\), and \({@a_sq@} = {@a@}^2\) is a perfect square:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \sqrt{{@n@}} = \sqrt{{@a_sq@} \times {@b@}} = \sqrt{{@a_sq@}} \times \sqrt{{@b@}} = {@a@}\sqrt{{@b@}}\)</p>

<p><b>Step 3: Multiply by \(2\) to find the doubled length in simplest form</b></p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \text{New length} = 2 \times {@a@}\sqrt{{@b@}} = {@ta1@}\text{ cm}\)</p>

<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \text{New length} = {@ta1@}\text{ cm} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
